{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1397 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4bfcda41d866c4e9a9414e899aae62d64df287e8912b7fe8d3056f4f8629d337" :edb/db-before-t 1396 :edb/db-after-t 1397 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534709 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:45:45.486Z" 13194139534709 true] [17592186100689 1145 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1,$ we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\nP_2(x) = f(1) + f'(1)(x-1) +\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(1)(x-1)^2.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1),$ $f'(1),$ and $f''(1)\\mathbin{;}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & f(1) & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(1) & =2{e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(1) & =4{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(1),$ $f'(1),$ and $f''(1)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+2{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe plot below shows $f(x),$ $P_2(x),$ and $L(x).$ We can see that $P_2(x)$ is a better approximation to $f(x)$ around $x=1$ compared with $L(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039968/assets/a72eb0b9a50cdc0f8867b6f86e9273fb86502ad6060abffc34f32ae536722ac7.png)" 13194139534709 true] [17592186100689 1145 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1,$ we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\nP_2(x) = f(1) + f'(1)(x-1) +\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(1)(x-1)^2.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1),$ $f'(1),$ and $f''(1)\\mathbin{;}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & f(1) & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(1) & =2{e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(1) & =4{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(1),$ $f'(1),$ and $f''(1)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+2{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe plot below shows $f(x),$ $P_2(x),$ and $L(x).$ We can see that $P_2(x)$ is a better approximation to $f(x)$ around $x=1$ compared with $L(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030220/assets/a72eb0b9a50cdc0f8867b6f86e9273fb86502ad6060abffc34f32ae536722ac7.png)" 13194139534709 false] [17592186100843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304739 13194139534709 true] [17592186100843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304767 13194139534709 true] [17592186100843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304770 13194139534709 true] [17592186100843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304781 13194139534709 true] [17592186100852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304746 13194139534709 true] [17592186100852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304753 13194139534709 true] [17592186100852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304760 13194139534709 true] [17592186100852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304777 13194139534709 true] [17592186101114 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534709 true] [17592186101114 1100 "What is the coefficient of $(x + 1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = e^{3x}$ at $x =-1$?" 13194139534709 false] [17592186101114 1100 "What is the coefficient of $(x+1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{3x}$ at $x=-1?$" 13194139534709 true] [17592186101114 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-1).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1)=9{e}^{3(-1)}=9{e}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{9{e}^{-3}}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534709 true] [17592186304739 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304740 13194139534709 true] [17592186304739 1089 "q-125085" 13194139534709 true] [17592186304739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534709 true] [17592186304739 1100 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{5x}$ about $x=0$ is" 13194139534709 true] [17592186304739 1113 #uuid "443233e2-c490-5eba-a149-cc94e7ac316d" 13194139534709 true] [17592186304739 1145 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{5x} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =5{e}^{5x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =25{e}^{5x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+5x+\\frac{25}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304741 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304742 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304743 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304744 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304745 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1041 "selection" 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304745 13194139534709 true] [17592186304740 1110 #uuid "6da36dfe-c74c-5b8d-b8ef-ab3c570d22a3" 13194139534709 true] [17592186304741 1034 "1+5x+25{x}^{2}" 13194139534709 true] [17592186304741 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534709 true] [17592186304741 1111 #